  "query_preview": "..An active monsoon pattern is forecast to remain in\nplace across northern Arizona for the next several days as a\nridge of high pressure centered over Colorado continues to pump \ndeep sub-tropical moisture into the region. Precipitable water \n(PW) values are exceptionally high for mid-July, with guidance \nshowing an inch or more across northern Arizona, surface dew \npoints in the 50s at higher elevations, and dew points in the 60s \nat lower elevations. \n\nHigh-resolution model guidance indicates unusually high\ninstability today, with CAPE values, a measure of atmospheric \ninstability, ranging from 1000 to 2500 J/kg across the area. The \nhighest instability is concentrated along and south of the \nMogollon Rim. Coincident with the areas of highest instability, \npockets of 0-6km wind shear between 20 and 30 knots are forecast. \nThese shear values are higher than typically observed in mid- \nJuly, and is expected to enhance storm organization and the severe\nweather threat. As a result, we will be monitoring for flash \nflooding alongside a moderate risk for powerful storms capable of \nproducing large hail and damaging wind gusts. Convective \ndevelopment is expected to increase over the higher terrain \naround noon before storms drift westward at 5-10 mph. \n\nThe monsoon high pressure cell is forecast to remain relatively \nstationary over the next few days, ensuring that deep moisture \nstays locked in place across northern Arizona. On Tuesday, an \ninverted trough will move along the western side of the ridge and\ntrack across Arizona, passing overhead on Wednesday. This feature\nmay provide additional dynamical lift to aid convective \ndevelopment.\n\nFrom Thursday onward, a shift in the synoptic pattern is \nanticipated as the majority of model guidance shows the monsoon \nhigh pressure cell strengthening and migrating westward. This \nretrograding position will bring warming aloft, resulting in more\nstable conditions. Under this scenario, we would see a downward \ntrend in storm activity and a gradual warming trend through the \nend of the week. However, this shift will not bring a complete \nshutdown in monsoon activity. The extended forecast maintains \nprobability of precipitation values of 30-50% over the higher \nterrain, and 30% or less for the lower elevations.",
